  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 243 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry weather, slightly below average \ntemperatures, and lower humidity today ahead of an unsettled period. \n\nLow pressure is slowly drifting off the Northeast coast, with a weak \nsurface high across the local area this morning. Generally light \nsoutherly winds and temperatures in the upper 60s to lower 70s are \nbeing observed across the area. Satellite imagery is highlighting \nupper-level cirrus spreading across the East Coast that is emanating \noff of another low pressure system centered across Illinois. This \ntrough will be one of the focal points for weather in the coming \ndays, but dry conditions will continue today. Upper heights are \nforecast to build a bit as the ridge and aforementioned surface high \nnudge offshore and the low-level flow southerly flow begins to \nincrease. Highs near 90F with afternoon dew points rising into the \nmid-upper 60s will make for a slightly more humid, warmer day than \nwe have seen the past few days.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2....A more typical summertime pattern returns by late \nthis weekend and early next week, with near average temperatures and \nincreasing shower/storm chances starting Sunday and continuing \nthrough early next week.\n\nThe pattern becomes more unsettled by Sunday will continue into \nearly next week as a slow moving upper-level trough approaches from \nthe west. At the same time, a weak cold front approaches from the \nnorth, but will remain north of the local area through Monday (and \nlikely wash out by Tue). With the approaching trough and Bermuda \nhigh pressure well offshore, deep-layered S to SW flow will prevail. \nThis will allow rich Gulf moisture to overspread the area on Sunday \nand remain over us early next week. PWs surge from a comfortable 1-\n1.4\" on Saturday to 2.2-2.4\" by late Sunday/Sunday night. Scattered \nto potentially numerous showers/tstms are expected Sunday. The \nhighest precip chances will initially be across south-central VA \nduring the afternoon (as the deep moisture arrives here first). \nShowers and tstms should gradually spread NE through the evening \n(though it may very well remain dry near the immediate coast and on \nthe eastern shore). Showers/tstms are likely across the entire area \non Monday as a lead shortwave potentially tracks over the area. \nMonday appears to be the day with the highest convective coverage \nduring the period. The trough dampens a bit from Tuesday/Wednesday, \nbut scattered showers and tstms are still expected during the \naftn/evening on both days as weak SW flow aloft continues and deep \nmoisture remains nearby. Given the environment and relatively weak \nunidirectional flow aloft and tall/skinny CAPE, there will be a \nthreat of localized flash flooding each day, but the severe threat \nshould remain relatively low with these storms. Temperatures dip \ndown slightly below average during the early week period thanks to \nthe cloud cover and multiple rounds of rain. Though not quite as \nunsettled as the first half of the week, the latter half of the week \nwill see isolated to scattered afternoon showers and thunderstorms. \nWith the decreased coverage forecast, temperatures will return to \nnear to slightly above normal with highs in the upper 80s to lower \n90s.",
